>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

I implemented Method 2 from the project specs: instead of manually
walking the page directory in software, I make sure the pointer is
below PHYS_BASE and let the CPU's MMU catch bad accesses via page
faults.

Here is how the pieces fit together:

- get_user() and put_user(): These use the inline assembly snippets
  from the Pintos manual. Before attempting the read or write, the code
  puts the address of an error-recovery label (1:) into EAX.
- page_fault() modification: In exception.c, I added a check for when
  the fault happens in kernel mode on a user virtual address
  (!user && is_user_vaddr(fault_addr)). When that occurs, the handler
  modifies the interrupt frame: it sets eip to the recovery address
  stored in eax and sets eax to -1. That causes execution to resume
  right past the faulting assembly instruction with an error code.
- read_user_byte(): The one place the actual check is written. It
  checks is_user_vaddr(), then calls get_user(), and kills the process
  with sys_exit(-1) if either fails. All the helpers below read user
  memory through it.
- check_user_byte(): Reads the byte with read_user_byte(), and if we
  need write permission, writes it back with put_user(). If the write
  faults, it kills the process with sys_exit(-1).
- get_arg(): Reads the syscall arguments off the user stack
  (f->esp + n * 4). Because an argument might sit right across a page
  boundary, it reads all 4 bytes one at a time with read_user_byte()
  and builds the 32-bit word.
- check_user_string(): Used for filenames and command strings. It walks
  byte-by-byte with read_user_byte() until it hits '\0', so any bad
  byte kills the process.
- check_user_buffer(): Used for buffers in read and write. It verifies
  that buffer + size doesn't wrap around or hit kernel space, checks
  the start byte, and then checks one byte on each subsequent page
  boundary the buffer touches. Testing write permission with put_user()
  also catches attempts to write to read-only code pages.

Once a buffer passes these checks, the kernel can safely read or write
it directly with functions like file_read() and file_write().

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

My implementation doesn't call pagedir_get_page() at all because the
hardware MMU handles address translation. But in terms of page-level
probes in check_user_buffer():

- 4,096-byte copy: If the buffer is page-aligned, it sits on exactly
  one page, so it needs 1 check. If it starts mid-page and spans across
  two pages, it touches both and needs 2 checks.
- 2-byte copy: It needs 1 check if both bytes are on the same page, or
  2 checks if the 2 bytes happen to straddle a page boundary.

This is the theoretical minimum. You cannot do fewer than one check per
page touched, because you have to verify every distinct physical page
the buffer uses. Checking every single byte would be redundant and
waste cycles.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

The wait(pid) syscall simply forwards to process_wait(pid):

  1. It searches the calling thread's children list for a
     struct child_info matching that pid.
  2. If it can't find one, it returns -1 immediately. That naturally
     takes care of invalid PIDs, processes that aren't direct children,
     and children that have already been waited on.
  3. If it finds the record, it calls sema_down(&child->exited). If the
     child is still running, the parent goes to sleep. If the child has
     already exited, sema_down returns right away since the semaphore
     remembers the up.
  4. The parent grabs child->exit_status, removes the child from
     children (so a second wait on the same PID will fail with -1), and
     drops its reference with release_child_info(). Then it returns the
     exit status.

When a child dies, process_exit() handles the other side:

- It prints the required exit message "name: exit(status)".
- It copies its exit_status into self_info->exit_status.
- It calls sema_up(&self_info->exited) to wake up the parent if it's
  waiting.
- It drops its reference to self_info.

Because exit_status is initialized to -1 in init_thread(), if a process
gets killed abruptly by the kernel (like on a page fault), it
automatically exits with -1 without any extra special-case code.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

I separated validation from the actual system call work to keep the
code clean:

1. Validation at the entry point: Inside syscall_handler(), I validate
   all arguments before calling the helper that actually performs the
   syscall. get_arg() pulls parameters from the stack, and
   check_user_string() / check_user_buffer() inspect pointers. If
   anything is invalid, they call sys_exit(-1) right then and there and
   never return. This means the actual syscall functions (sys_write,
   sys_open, etc.) can assume all incoming pointers and arguments are
   100% valid, keeping their implementation simple and readable.

2. Strict ordering prevents resource leaks: I make sure that all user
   pointer validation happens before acquiring filesys_lock or
   allocating any memory with malloc(). If a user passes a garbage
   buffer, the process terminates before taking the lock or allocating
   an entry. This guarantees we never leak a held lock on a bad pointer
   fault.

3. Centralized cleanup in process_exit(): Any process termination,
   whether calling exit(), faulting on a bad pointer, or dying to an
   exception, always runs through process_exit(). That single function
   cleans up all process resources: closes all open files in
   open_files, closes the running executable to re-enable writes, frees
   the page directory, wakes up any waiting parent, and cleans up child
   records.

Example: write(fd, buffer, size)

In syscall_handler:

  1. get_arg() reads fd, buffer, and size. If the user's stack pointer
     is invalid, it dies here.
  2. check_user_buffer(buffer, size, false) probes each page of the
     buffer. If an unmapped address is passed, it dies here.
  3. Only after both checks pass does sys_write() run, acquire
     filesys_lock, and call file_write(). Because the buffer was
     already verified, file_write() won't page-fault while holding the
     file system lock.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

In process_execute(), I allocate a struct exec_info directly on the
parent's stack. It contains the command line string, a pointer to the
shared child_info, a loaded semaphore initialized to 0, and a boolean
success flag. I pass this struct as the aux argument to
thread_create().

Immediately after calling thread_create(), the parent calls
sema_down(&exec.loaded) and blocks.

The child thread starts up in start_process(), unpacks exec_info, and
calls load(). Once load() finishes, the child writes the result into
exec->success and calls sema_up(&exec->loaded). If loading failed, the
child calls thread_exit().

The sema_up wakes the parent. The parent checks exec.success: if true,
it returns the child's TID; if false, it cleans up and returns
TID_ERROR (-1).

Stack-allocating exec_info on the parent is completely safe here
because the parent is blocked on the semaphore the entire time the
child is loading. As soon as the child calls sema_up, it never touches
exec_info again.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

To handle synchronization without memory leaks or use-after-free bugs,
I use a heap-allocated struct child_info with a reference count
(ref_cnt = 2), shared between parent P and child C. Each side drops its
reference with release_child_info(), and whichever side drops the count
to 0 frees the memory. I disable interrupts during the decrement to
ensure reference counting is atomic.

Here is how each scenario behaves:

1. P waits before C exits: P calls sema_down(&child->exited) and
   sleeps. When C exits, process_exit() stores its exit status, calls
   sema_up(&child->exited) (waking P), and calls release_child_info()
   (ref_cnt becomes 1). P wakes up, reads exit_status, and calls
   release_child_info() (ref_cnt hits 0 and P frees the struct).

2. P waits after C exits: C has already exited, saved its exit code,
   upped the semaphore, and dropped its reference (ref_cnt = 1). When P
   eventually calls wait(), sema_down returns immediately because the
   semaphore remembers the up. P reads the status and calls
   release_child_info() (ref_cnt hits 0 and P frees the struct).

3. P exits before C exits: P's process_exit() loops through its
   children list and drops its reference on every child record (ref_cnt
   drops to 1). C keeps executing normally as an orphan. When C finally
   exits, its call to release_child_info() drops ref_cnt from 1 to 0
   and frees the struct.

4. P exits after C exits without waiting: C already exited and dropped
   its reference (ref_cnt = 1). When P exits without calling wait(),
   its process_exit() drops the remaining reference on C's record,
   bringing ref_cnt to 0 and freeing it.

Corner cases:

- Child load fails: If load() fails, C calls thread_exit(), dropping
  its reference. The parent notices exec.success == false in
  process_execute(), calls release_child_info() to drop the parent's
  reference, and returns TID_ERROR without adding C to its children
  list.
- thread_create() fails: If the thread can't even be created,
  process_execute() immediately frees the command line page and the
  child_info struct directly.
- Thread struct cleanup: The child's struct thread is reclaimed by the
  scheduler as soon as the thread dies; only the lightweight child_info
  struct stays alive until both sides are done with it.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

I chose Method 2 because it's both faster and closer to how real
operating systems like Linux work.

1. Performance: In the vast majority of cases, user pointers are
   completely valid. Walking page directories manually in software with
   pagedir_get_page() adds overhead to every single byte or word read.
   With Method 2, you just verify is_user_vaddr() and let the CPU's MMU
   translate the address in hardware at full speed.

2. True permissions checking: Software lookups often overlook write
   permissions on read-only pages (like trying to read() into a code
   segment). put_user() performs an actual write cycle, so if the page
   is write-protected, the MMU faults immediately and we catch it
   cleanly.

3. Simplicity: It required very little code: just the inline assembly
   wrappers in syscall.c and a short check in page_fault() to redirect
   EIP to the recovery label.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages:

- Dynamic memory usage: Using a linked list inside struct thread means
  processes only use memory for the files they actually have open. If
  I used a fixed array like struct file *fds[128], it would bloat
  struct thread, which is dangerous because in Pintos the thread struct
  and the kernel stack share a single 4 kB page.
- No stale descriptor bugs: Incrementing next_fd monotonically ensures
  that if a process closes fd 3 and opens another file, the new file
  gets fd 4. It won't accidentally reuse fd 3 right away, making it
  easier to catch use-after-close bugs.
- Independent file offsets: Each open() call allocates a separate
  struct open_file with its own struct file *, so two open instances of
  the same file maintain their own independent file positions.

Disadvantages:

- O(n) lookups: Finding an fd requires traversing the linked list. For
  Pintos test cases this is plenty fast, but for real-world servers
  opening thousands of files, an array or hash table with O(1) lookups
  would be much better.
- No fd recycling: Since next_fd only counts upward, an application
  running for months opening and closing files repeatedly could
  theoretically wrap around an integer.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

I kept the default 1:1 identity mapping where pid == tid. In Pintos at
this stage, each process is strictly single-threaded, so a thread and a
process are effectively the same thing. Adding a separate data
structure or translation table to map PIDs to TIDs would add
unnecessary complexity without any real benefit. A separate mapping
table would only be useful if Pintos supported multithreaded user
processes where several threads share a single PID.

